The proposed architecture uses the Strangler Fig migration pattern: gradually extract bounded capabilities from the Python monolith while maintaining existing functionality, preserving data integrity, and enabling independent deployment.

The graph is designed to prevent downstream work from proceeding when critical dependencies have not passed verification.

1. Migration strategy

The migration will favor:

Business-capability boundaries over arbitrary module splits.

Contract-first APIs and explicit data ownership.

Independently deployable services, with shared infrastructure where justified.

Parallel development only after interfaces and ownership are accepted.

Canary releases, telemetry, and rehearsed rollback before production cutover.

Assumptions: Existing Python application and tests are available later; service boundaries, infrastructure, database technology, SLOs, and compliance requirements remain to be discovered. The initial graph is technology-neutral and does not assume Kubernetes, Kafka, or a particular Python web framework.

