A05 is a repeatable service-extraction subgraph, not a single uncontrolled batch. For each approved service, it contains extraction, isolated tests, contract verification, and packaging. Services can be built concurrently only when their dependencies and ownership boundaries permit it.

Parallel execution requires genuine worker dispatch and isolated workspaces. Otherwise, execution is sequential, without claiming independent agents.

4. Acceptance criteria and quality gates
The following are proposed initial thresholds. Production-specific SLOs, latency budgets, and allowable migration downtime must be confirmed during discovery, not invented.

Gate

Mandatory passing condition

Failure behavior

G0 · Baseline

Source inventory complete; baseline tests run; existing failures recorded

Block changes if baseline cannot be established

G1 · Discovery

Entrypoints, dependencies, database access, external integrations, and critical workflows mapped; unresolved high-risk gaps reviewed

Return to responsible discovery agent

G2 · Architecture

Service ownership unambiguous; API schemas validate; data ownership and consistency models approved; no prohibited circular dependencies

Block implementation

G3 · Integration

Required tests pass; contract compatibility verified; data reconciliation passes; no unaccepted critical security findings

Block release preparation

G4 · Release readiness

Rollback rehearsal passes; dashboards and alerts operational; canary and rollback thresholds approved; authorized operator signs off

Block production rollout

Service-level acceptance criteria
Every extracted service must demonstrate:

Functional parity: All agreed critical business scenarios pass against the baseline.

Contract integrity: OpenAPI or equivalent schemas validate; consumer-driven contract tests pass.

Data correctness: Agreed reconciliation invariants pass, with zero unexplained discrepancies in critical records.

Security: No unresolved critical vulnerabilities or unapproved high-severity findings.

Reliability: Service meets approved latency, availability, timeout, and error-budget targets.

Operability: Health checks, structured logs, metrics, distributed traces, and runbooks exist.

Deployability: Reproducible build, independently versioned deployment, and tested recovery path.

A gate reports PASS, FAIL, or UNRESOLVED. Missing evidence always produces UNRESOLVED, never PASS.

