Each retry must preserve the previous artifact, record the failure reason, change only the responsible node's scope, and re-run the original evaluator. A failed node never causes unrelated successful nodes to restart.

For database migration, rollback must distinguish between traffic reversal and data reversal. Traffic may be switched back only when the old application remains compatible with data written by the new service. Otherwise, recovery uses a separately approved reconciliation, replay, or forward-fix procedure. Destructive database rollback is not assumed safe.

7. Execution constraints and risk controls
Risk

Proposed control

Shared database coupling

Explicit ownership and incremental data separation

Breaking API changes

Versioned contracts and consumer compatibility tests

Distributed transaction failures

Idempotency, outbox/inbox patterns where appropriate, and reconciliation

Concurrent file conflicts

Separate branches/worktrees and single-writer ownership

Security regressions

Automated scanning and human review for high-risk changes

Production outage

Progressive routing, SLO-based abort, and verified recovery

Data loss

Backups, checkpoints, reconciliation, and restore rehearsal

No node may publish, spend money, send external communications, delete data, or modify production without separate explicit authorization. Where programmatic permission enforcement is unavailable, the limitation must be reported rather than treated as enforced isolation.

